From 73abdea53978f11f925a7e3238321c7395941123 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Micheal Waltz <micheal@ecliptik.com>
Date: Wed, 2 Sep 2026 19:43:20 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] dos: percentile fps telemetry (RUNMANIFEST fps_p50/fps_p95)

The hub's own benchmark skill requires a percentile-based KPI
(fps_p50/fps_p95 via shared/include/runmanifest.h), and dossage could
not produce one -- it only ever reported a single average
(frameCount/netTime). That is not a formality: the closing campaign on
the S3 ViRGE leg found a single average of 14.94 hiding a steady-state
p50 of ~15.0 next to a much lower p95, and would have shown that on the
very first run instead of after eight falsified hypotheses had a
percentile existed then. See docs/BENCHMARK-PLAN.md's "Pre-campaign
work item" section for the full story.

What this adds, in playGame():

- A bounded buffer (20000 doubles, ~22 minutes at 15fps) capturing the
  real per-frame duration (the existing `frameTime` local) once per loop
  iteration, before any pacing/sleep is applied that frame. Recording
  stops rather than overflowing past the cap.

- At the same clean-exit point the existing average-fps print already
  uses, sort the captured buffer and take fps_p50 = 1/sorted[floor(0.50
  * (n-1))], fps_p95 = 1/sorted[floor(0.95 * (n-1))]. Left at
  runmanifest_init()'s -1.0 NA sentinel when the buffer is empty (n==0),
  rather than dividing by zero.

  The inversion is deliberate, not a bug: p95 is taken over per-frame
  TIME, so it names the slow tail (95% of frames were at least this
  fast, 5% were slower), which inverts to the LOW end of the fps range,
  below p50 -- not above it.

- Emit a RUNMANIFEST block (schema v1) to RUNMANI.LOG via
  runmanifest_emit(), alongside (not replacing) the existing printf.
  Fields filled from real data: schema_version, environment
  (runmanifest_detect_environment(), DJGPP-only), binary_sha12 (from the
  new PORT_BUILD_SHA12 macro, a companion Makefile change), duration_s
  (the existing netTime), exit_code (0, the one clean-exit path this
  hooks), fps_p50, fps_p95. Everything else (started_utc, wave_tag,
  scene, env_block_sha, audio_vital_status, regime, banner/critical/warn
  counters) stays at runmanifest_init()'s NA/0/empty defaults -- dossage
  has no infra to populate those honestly yet.

Reject implausible frame-time samples before they reach the percentile
buffer (added after a build-qa DOSBox-X smoke run on an earlier version
of this patch caught it for real):

A natural 298s life (age -> death -> fade) came back with
fps_p50=13114403.96 -- a nonsensical ~13 million fps, i.e. the sorted
sample at the p50 index was ~7.6e-8s (76ns). frameTimeSampleCount
matched frameCount exactly (4475), ruling out a double-sampling or
count-mismatch bug -- every captured sample corresponds to a real
counted frame. That means roughly half of ALL captured frame-time
samples in that life were reading as near-zero.

Ruled out, by direct calculation, not assumption: pure floating-point
rounding of Time::getCurrentTime()'s millisecond-quantized inputs
(currentTimeS + currentTimeMS/1000.0, both integer-valued components)
cannot produce a standalone nonzero result smaller than a genuine
millisecond-scale difference -- simulating the actual arithmetic across
every possible whole-millisecond pairing near a realistic epoch value
only ever yields exactly 0.0 (identical inputs) or the real, correctly-
scaled difference. A 40-70ms real per-frame duration (documented
repeatedly elsewhere in this file via direct real-hardware measurement)
cannot collapse to 76ns through float noise alone.

Leading hypothesis, not yet confirmed by a dedicated probe: this file's
own pacer history already documents DJGPP's timekeeping as capable of
losing ticks under insufficient yielding -- the reverted busy-wait
experiment commented on above measured time(NULL) itself advancing only
41s across a ~90s run (54% of ticks lost) when the loop stopped
yielding. Time::getCurrentTime() likely derives from the same
periodically-serviced BIOS tick counter. A stalled tick would read back
near-identical to the previous call (near-zero delta), then "catch up"
on the next real tick (a compensating large delta) -- exactly the
alternation implied by the data (frameCount/netTime's coarse average
stayed a normal 15.017fps across the whole 298s life, meaning the large
compensating frames account for essentially all of the real elapsed
time). This needs a standalone probe (isolated gettimeofday() call-rate
characterization, not embedded in the full game) to confirm rather than
infer from the game's own aggregate behavior -- flagged, not
investigated further here.

The fix here is deliberately narrow and independent of which hypothesis
is right: Time::getCurrentTime() truncates to whole milliseconds
(TimeDOS.cpp's tv_usec/1000, see the pacer's own comment on this a few
lines up), and no real frame here can do render + audio-pump + game
logic work in under a millisecond -- so any captured delta that is <= 0
or below one clock tick is definitionally not a measurement of real
work, regardless of why the clock produced it. Such samples are now
excluded from the percentile buffer rather than corrupting it, and
counted (not silently dropped) via a new printf alongside the existing
average-fps print, so a lopsided reject count stays visible data for
whoever looks at the next run's output rather than a hidden correction.

Only the one clean-exit path is covered, matching the existing average
print's own single call site -- catching every abnormal exit is out of
scope for this patch.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01ULWUsqCW7jJL5TdiFQMJwf
---
 gameSource/game.cpp | 154 ++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++--
 1 file changed, 149 insertions(+), 5 deletions(-)

diff --git a/gameSource/game.cpp b/gameSource/game.cpp
index 7034511..270d5cd 100644
--- a/gameSource/game.cpp
+++ b/gameSource/game.cpp
@@ -52,6 +52,55 @@ int main( int inArgCount, char **inArgs ) {
 #include "minorGems/util/stringUtils.h"
 #include "minorGems/util/SettingsManager.h"
 
+#ifdef __DJGPP__
+// DOS-PORT: percentile fps telemetry (RUNMANIFEST fps_p50/fps_p95). See
+// playGame()'s frame-time sample buffer (where captured) and its exit
+// block (where summarized and emitted) for the rest of this feature.
+#include "runmanifest.h"
+
+// 20000 entries ~= 22 minutes at 15fps -- comfortably longer than any
+// planned benchmark run. Capture stops rather than overflowing if a run
+// somehow exceeds this.
+#define DOS_PORT_FRAME_TIME_SAMPLE_CAP 20000
+
+// DOS-PORT: floor below which a captured frameTime cannot be a real
+// measurement, derived from the clock's own documented granularity
+// (Time::getCurrentTime() truncates to whole milliseconds -- TimeDOS.cpp's
+// tv_usec/1000, see the pacer's own comment on this above), not from any
+// assumption about how fast this game's per-frame work can get. A real
+// build-qa DOSBox-X run found roughly half of all captured samples in a
+// long life reading as low as ~7.6e-8s (a nonsensical ~13 million fps) --
+// confirmed by direct calculation that pure floating-point rounding of
+// two millisecond-quantized instants cannot produce a nonzero result
+// smaller than a genuine millisecond-scale difference on its own, so a
+// value this far below one clock tick means the underlying clock read
+// something non-monotonic or stale, not that a frame actually ran in
+// under a microsecond. Leading hypothesis (not yet confirmed by a
+// dedicated probe): DJGPP's gettimeofday() derives from the same
+// periodically-serviced BIOS tick counter this file's own pacer history
+// already documents as capable of stalling under insufficient yielding
+// (see the reverted busy-wait experiment's comment above, which measured
+// time(NULL) itself losing 54% of ticks on real hardware) -- a stalled
+// tick would read back near-identical to the previous call, then "catch
+// up" on the next real tick, which would produce exactly this near-zero/
+// compensating-large alternation. Filtering rather than trusting these
+// blindly. 0.001s (1ms) matches that floor without needing to assume
+// anything about this game's own performance profile.
+#define DOS_PORT_FRAME_TIME_MIN_PLAUSIBLE_S 0.001
+
+static int dosPortCompareFrameTimes( const void *inA, const void *inB ) {
+    double a = *(const double *)inA;
+    double b = *(const double *)inB;
+    if( a < b ) {
+        return -1;
+        }
+    if( a > b ) {
+        return 1;
+        }
+    return 0;
+    }
+#endif
+
 
 
 // size of game image
@@ -1082,7 +1131,29 @@ char playGame() {
 
     int frameCount = 0;
     unsigned long startTime = time( NULL );
-    
+
+#ifdef __DJGPP__
+    // DOS-PORT: scoped identically to frameCount/startTime just above --
+    // mainFunction()'s while(playGame()) loop can call this function many
+    // times per session (one per life), same as the existing average-fps
+    // print in this function's exit block below.
+    //
+    // static (BSS), not on the stack: 20000 doubles is 160000 bytes.
+    // frameTimeSampleCount (an ordinary local, reset to 0 every call) is
+    // what actually bounds how much of the array is valid data for THIS
+    // life -- leftover content past that count, from a previous life, is
+    // never read.
+    static double frameTimeSamples[ DOS_PORT_FRAME_TIME_SAMPLE_CAP ];
+    int frameTimeSampleCount = 0;
+
+    // Counts of samples excluded as implausible -- see
+    // DOS_PORT_FRAME_TIME_MIN_PLAUSIBLE_S's own comment for why. Reported
+    // (not silently dropped) in this function's exit block, so a lopsided
+    // count is visible data rather than a hidden correction.
+    int frameTimeRejectedNonPositive = 0;
+    int frameTimeRejectedTooSmall = 0;
+#endif
+
     int titleFadeFrame = 0;
     int numTitleFadeFrames = 200;
 
@@ -1541,7 +1612,25 @@ char playGame() {
         double newTimestamp = Time::getCurrentTime();
         
         double frameTime = newTimestamp - lastFrameTimeStamp;
-        
+
+#ifdef __DJGPP__
+        // DOS-PORT: capture the real per-frame duration for the
+        // RUNMANIFEST percentile computation, before any pacing/sleep
+        // below adjusts anything -- see this function's exit block.
+        // Reject implausible readings rather than trusting the clock
+        // blindly -- see DOS_PORT_FRAME_TIME_MIN_PLAUSIBLE_S's own comment.
+        if( frameTime <= 0.0 ) {
+            frameTimeRejectedNonPositive ++;
+            }
+        else if( frameTime < DOS_PORT_FRAME_TIME_MIN_PLAUSIBLE_S ) {
+            frameTimeRejectedTooSmall ++;
+            }
+        else if( frameTimeSampleCount < DOS_PORT_FRAME_TIME_SAMPLE_CAP ) {
+            frameTimeSamples[ frameTimeSampleCount ] = frameTime;
+            frameTimeSampleCount ++;
+            }
+#endif
+
         double extraTime = 1.0 / lockedFrameRate - frameTime;
         
         // DOS-PORT: SDL_Delay, not Thread::staticSleep -- on DOS,
@@ -2118,12 +2207,67 @@ char playGame() {
     printf( "Frame rate = %f fps (%d frames)\n", 
             frameRate, frameCount );
 
-    printf( "Game time = %d:%d\n", 
+    printf( "Game time = %d:%d\n",
             (int)netTime / 60, (int)netTime % 60 );
 
+#ifdef __DJGPP__
+    // DOS-PORT: visible, not silent -- a lopsided reject count is itself
+    // a finding (see DOS_PORT_FRAME_TIME_MIN_PLAUSIBLE_S's own comment),
+    // not something to correct quietly out of the percentile inputs.
+    printf( "Frame-time samples: %d used, %d rejected non-positive, "
+            "%d rejected below %.3fs\n",
+            frameTimeSampleCount, frameTimeRejectedNonPositive,
+            frameTimeRejectedTooSmall,
+            (double)DOS_PORT_FRAME_TIME_MIN_PLAUSIBLE_S );
+#endif
+
     fflush( stdout );
-    
-    
+
+#ifdef __DJGPP__
+    {
+        runmanifest_t manifest;
+        runmanifest_init( &manifest );
+
+        manifest.environment = runmanifest_detect_environment( NULL );
+
+#ifdef PORT_BUILD_SHA12
+        strncpy( manifest.binary_sha12, PORT_BUILD_SHA12,
+                 sizeof( manifest.binary_sha12 ) - 1 );
+        manifest.binary_sha12[ sizeof( manifest.binary_sha12 ) - 1 ] = '\0';
+#endif
+
+        manifest.duration_s = (int)netTime;
+        manifest.exit_code = 0;
+
+        if( frameTimeSampleCount > 0 ) {
+            qsort( frameTimeSamples, frameTimeSampleCount, sizeof( double ),
+                   dosPortCompareFrameTimes );
+
+            // p95 of frame TIME is the slow tail (95% of frames were at
+            // least this fast, 5% were slower) -- inverting it lands at
+            // the LOW end of the fps range, below p50, not above it. See
+            // this patch's own commit message for the motivating story.
+            int p50Index =
+                (int)( 0.50 * ( frameTimeSampleCount - 1 ) );
+            int p95Index =
+                (int)( 0.95 * ( frameTimeSampleCount - 1 ) );
+
+            if( frameTimeSamples[ p50Index ] > 0 ) {
+                manifest.fps_p50 = 1.0 / frameTimeSamples[ p50Index ];
+                }
+            if( frameTimeSamples[ p95Index ] > 0 ) {
+                manifest.fps_p95 = 1.0 / frameTimeSamples[ p95Index ];
+                }
+            }
+
+        FILE *runManifestLog = fopen( "RUNMANI.LOG", "a" );
+        if( runManifestLog != NULL ) {
+            runmanifest_emit( runManifestLog, &manifest );
+            fclose( runManifestLog );
+            }
+        }
+#endif
+
     delete titleImage;
 
     delete [] gameImage;
-- 
2.47.3

